## Proof

Let $P \subset S^2$ be the spherical $n$-gon, assumed to lie in an open hemisphere, with interior angles $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n$. Its boundary $\partial P$ consists of $n$ geodesic arcs (great-circle segments) meeting at $n$ vertices. We apply the **Gauss–Bonnet theorem** to the region $P$ on the unit sphere $S^2$.

### Setup

The unit sphere has Gaussian curvature $K \equiv 1$. The boundary $\partial P$ is piecewise geodesic, so the geodesic curvature $k_g$ vanishes along every smooth (edge) portion of $\partial P$. At each vertex the **exterior angle** (the turning angle of the tangent, measured in $(-\pi,\pi)$) is

$$\theta_i = \pi - \alpha_i,$$

where $\alpha_i$ is the interior angle. The region $P$ is topologically a closed disk, so its Euler characteristic is

$$\chi(P) = 1.$$

### Applying Gauss–Bonnet

For a compact region $R$ with piecewise-smooth boundary on an oriented surface, the Gauss–Bonnet theorem gives

$$\iint_R K\,dA \;+\; \oint_{\partial R} k_g\,ds \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{n} \theta_i \;=\; 2\pi\,\chi(R).$$

Substituting our data ($K=1$, $k_g=0$ on edges, $\theta_i = \pi-\alpha_i$, $\chi(P)=1$):

$$\iint_P 1\,dA \;+\; 0 \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{n}(\pi - \alpha_i) \;=\; 2\pi.$$

The first term is exactly the area $A = \operatorname{Area}(P)$. Hence

$$A \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{n}(\pi - \alpha_i) \;=\; 2\pi.$$

### Solving for the area

$$A \;=\; 2\pi - \sum_{i=1}^{n}(\pi - \alpha_i) \;=\; 2\pi - n\pi + \sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_i \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_i - (n-2)\pi.$$

### Role of the hemisphere hypothesis

The hemisphere condition guarantees the polygon bounds a **unique** simply connected region $P$ that is the "small" one (the one contained in that hemisphere), so the exterior angles $\theta_i = \pi - \alpha_i$ all lie in $(-\pi,\pi)$ and the standard orientation of $\partial P$ applies. This removes the ambiguity that would otherwise arise from the complementary region (which also has geodesic boundary but area $4\pi - A$). With $P$ confined to an open hemisphere, $A < 2\pi$, consistent with the formula above.

### Conclusion

The area of a spherical $n$-gon on the unit sphere, contained in an open hemisphere, with interior angles $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_n$, is

$$\boxed{\,\operatorname{Area}(P) \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_i \;-\; (n-2)\pi\,}.$$

This is the **spherical excess** (generalized Girard theorem): the area equals the excess of the angle sum over the Euclidean value $(n-2)\pi$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
